// Stratified negation-as-failure: a block can negate a derived predicate with
// `not pred(v)`. The planner inlines the predicate's body and wraps it in a
// set-difference (`factstore.Not`), so "not overdue" reads as the complement of
// the derivation — no host glue, no second query.
//
//   ./tln build examples/negation.tln
//   ./tln test  examples/negation.tln test/negation.tln.test
//
// Negation is *stratified*: a derived predicate may not depend on its own
// negation (`derive p(v) { ... not p(v) }` is rejected — a predicate that is
// true exactly when it is false has no stratified meaning). The validator
// tags derive→derive edges with polarity and rejects a cycle that crosses a
// negative edge with a distinct "not stratifiable" error. Negation *through*
// recursion, which needs well-founded semantics to mean anything, is tracked
// in issue #170.

derive overdue(v) {
  for records where type == "vehicle"
    and attr "km" > attr "last_service_km" + 20000
}

// Vehicles that are NOT overdue — the stratified complement of `overdue`.
detect "Up to date" {
  for records where type == "vehicle"
    and status == "active"
    and not overdue(v)
  flag matching items
  label "{item.name}: service up to date ({attr.km} km)"
  priority LOW
}
